## Modern Applications (Post-2020s Additions)

The original lecture predates several major shifts in ML. Worth adding to the picture:

**Generative AI & Large Language Models**
- Conversational assistants (like the one you're reading this from), code generation and pair-programming tools, AI-powered search and research assistants
- Document/report drafting, summarization, translation at near-human quality
- Multimodal models that understand and generate text, images, audio, and video together

**Image & video generation**
- Text-to-image and text-to-video generation for marketing, design prototyping, and content creation
- AI-assisted photo/video editing (background removal, upscaling, style transfer)

**Healthcare**
- Medical imaging diagnostics (detecting tumors, fractures, diabetic retinopathy from scans)
- Drug discovery acceleration — predicting molecule behavior instead of pure lab trial-and-error
- Personalized treatment recommendations based on patient history and genomics

**Autonomous systems**
- Self-driving cars and driver-assist systems (beyond just factory automation)
- Drones for delivery, agriculture monitoring, and surveillance
- Warehouse robotics (e.g., automated picking and sorting at scale)

**Code & software engineering**
- AI coding agents that can write, review, debug, and even autonomously execute multi-step engineering tasks
- Automated test generation and code review assistants

**Fraud & security**
- Real-time transaction fraud detection (well beyond the loan-approval use case)
- Cybersecurity threat detection and anomaly detection in network traffic
- Deepfake detection — an ML application that exists specifically *because* generative ML created a new problem

**Personalization at scale**
- Recommendation engines have evolved from basic collaborative filtering to deep learning-based systems (Netflix, Spotify, YouTube, e-commerce) that personalize in near real-time
- Dynamic pricing has expanded well beyond ride-hailing into airlines, hotels, and e-commerce flash sales

**Agentic AI**
- Systems that don't just answer questions but autonomously plan and execute multi-step tasks — booking, research, data pipeline management, customer support resolution end-to-end
- AI agents integrated into business tools (CRM, project management, spreadsheets) to automate workflows directly, rather than just assisting a human doing them

**Climate & scientific research**
- Weather and climate modeling improvements
- Protein structure prediction (a major scientific breakthrough enabling faster biology/medical research)
- Materials science — discovering new materials computationally before physical synthesis

---

## Key Takeaway

The original lecture's core lens — **ML quietly running inside a business to drive profit (B2B) vs. ML you directly interact with (B2C)** — still holds. What's changed since is the *scale and autonomy*: ML has moved from narrow prediction tasks (will this loan default? which product to stock?) toward generative and agentic systems that can produce content and take actions on their own.
